Subject: Sample functions on wall time, for the per-function table

What this does
  Adds an optional sampler (QJS_FNSAMPLE=1) that takes a SIGPROF sample every
  200 us, records which bytecode function was innermost, and keeps each
  sample's program counter so the reader can say which kind of time it was.
  The table is written to QJS_FNSAMPLE_OUT when the runtime goes away.

Why we need it
  The compiled build cannot be improved without knowing which functions the
  time is in and, for each, how much of that time is inside the generated code,
  in an AOT helper, in the interpreter running a function the emitter did not
  compile, or in engine work such as GC. Whole-program timing separates none of
  these, and the existing per-op counters weight by op count rather than by
  time. A single in-unit bit is not enough either: it counts helper time and
  interpreter time as "engine", which are exactly what a better emitter removes.

  The period has to be the one that was asked for, or a function's share of the
  samples is not its share of the run. `setitimer` rounds up to the kernel tick
  (1 ms here), and a POSIX timer on CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID is clamped to the
  same tick: a 200 us request is delivered every ~1000 us, measured. The same
  request on CLOCK_MONOTONIC is delivered every 200 us, so the timer runs on
  wall time. The cost is that a sample taken while the process is not running
  JS is counted as no-JS rather than deferred; that count is written out so the
  bias is visible, and so is the wall time covered, so a reader can verify
  total samples x period instead of assuming it.

What it adds
  QJS_FNSAMPLE               - 0 by default; 1 builds the sampler
  QJS_FNSAMPLE_OUT           - the table's path; unset means no sampling
  fn_sample_tab[]            - 8192 open-addressed entries, no allocation
  fn_sample_pcs[]            - a 2^20 ring of {slot, pc}, filled by the handler
  T <total> <nojs>           - sample counts
  W <seconds>                - wall time the table covers
  B <hex> <object>           - a load base per mapped object (exe, libm,
                               libc, ld.so), for resolving PCs
  F <samples> <in_unit> key=<k> state=<bound|disabled|absent|unbound> <name>
    <file>:<line>:<col>
  P <key> <pc hex>           - one per sample, key 0xffffffff for no-JS
  O <dropped>                - samples the ring could not hold

  The `state` field is not a boolean because "no native entry" has three
  causes: the function was never offered (unbound), it is not in the unit
  (absent), or it was bound and then switched off (disabled). Only the last is
  an exit-policy outcome, so the reader can tell a function the emitter failed
  to cover from one the old policy gave up on.

Bytecode version
  No.

